  "nl_text": "The schemabench_0053 database contains nine tables, each representing a different entity or relationship in the system. The tables are:\n1. canvas_posts: This table stores information about blog posts, including the post's ID, slug, title, summary, body, published date, featured image, and user ID. The table also includes columns for created and updated timestamps, as well as a deleted timestamp. The primary key is the ID column, and the table has a foreign key relationship with the users table.\n2. canvas_posts_tags: This table stores information about the tags associated with each blog post. Each row in this table represents a tag and the post it is associated with. The table has a foreign key relationship with the canvas_tags table.\n3. canvas_posts_topics: This table stores information about the topics associated with each blog post. Each row in this table represents a topic and the post it is associated with. The table has a foreign key relationship with the canvas_topics table.\n4. canvas_tags: This table stores information about the tags used in the blog. Each row in this table represents a tag and its associated slug, name, and user ID. The primary key is the ID column, and the table has a foreign key relationship with the users table.\n5. canvas_topics: This table stores information about the topics used in the blog. Each row in this table represents a topic and its associated slug, name, and user ID. The primary key is the ID column, and the table has a foreign key relationship with the users table.\n6. canvas_user_meta: This table stores information about the users of the blog, including their username, summary, avatar, dark mode preference, digest preference, locale, and created and updated timestamps. The primary key is the ID column, and the table has a foreign key relationship with the users table.\n7. canvas_views: This table stores information about the views of each blog post, including the post's ID, IP address, user agent, referer, and created and updated timestamps. The primary key is the ID column, and the table has a foreign key relationship with the canvas_posts table.\n8. canvas_visits: This table stores information about the visits to each blog post, including the post's ID, IP address, user agent, referer, and created and updated timestamps. The primary key is the ID column, and the table has a foreign key relationship with the canvas_posts table.\n9. users: This table stores information about the users of the blog, including their name, email, email verification status, password, and created and updated timestamps. The primary key is the ID column.\nIn summary, the schemabench_0053 database contains tables that represent blog posts, tags, topics, users, and views and visits. The tables are related to each other through foreign key relationships, and the data is organized in a way that allows for efficient querying and retrieval.",
  "gold_question": "Laravel Publishing Platform: Canvas\n\n 1) Background / Context\nCanvas is an open-source blogging package designed to integrate seamlessly into existing Laravel applications. Inspired by Medium, the platform offers a clean, distraction-free writing environment and advanced analytics to help users understand content performance and reader engagement.\n\n 2) Objectives\nThe primary objective of Canvas is to provide a robust and user-friendly blogging platform that can be easily incorporated into a Laravel project. It aims to facilitate content creation, management, and detailed analytics on user interaction.\n\n 3) Stakeholders & Users\n- Administrators: Manage overall platform settings, user roles, and permissions.\n- Editors: Publish and manage posts (including those of other users).\n- Contributors: Create and manage their own posts, without publishing rights.\n- Readers: Access published content and contribute to viewing statistics.\n\n 4) Functional Descriptions\n- User Management: \n  - Default admin user setup on installation.\n  - CRUD operations for user profiles and role assignments.\n\n- Content Management:\n  - Create, edit, and delete posts with features like tagging and categorization by topics.\n  - Schedule posts with publishing dates.\n\n- Roles and Permissions:\n  - Three predefined roles: Contributor, Editor, Admin.\n  - Role-specific access for content creation, management, and publication.\n\n- Analytics:\n  - Track monthly trends and reader traffic.\n  - Collect and display post view data based on IP, user agent, and referer.\n\n 5) Non-Functional Descriptions\n- Performance: The platform should efficiently support typical load usage scenarios consistent with medium-sized blogging environments.\n- Usability: The interface should be intuitive and easy to navigate for all roles.\n- Security: Ensure secure user authentication and data protection, especially regarding publish and view analytics data linkage.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_codellama13b/0053.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0053\nCREATE TABLE `canvas_posts` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `slug` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `title` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `summary` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `body` text NOT NULL,\n  `published_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `featured_image` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  `deleted_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `canvas_posts_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `canvas_posts_tags` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `post_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `tag_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `canvas_posts_tags_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`post_id`) REFERENCES `canvas_posts` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `canvas_posts_tags_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`tag_id`) REFERENCES `canvas_tags` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `canvas_posts_topics` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `post_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `topic_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `canvas_posts_topics_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`post_id`) REFERENCES `canvas_posts` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `canvas_posts_topics_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`topic_id`) REFERENCES `canvas_topics` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `canvas_tags` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `slug` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `canvas_tags_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `canvas_topics` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `slug` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `canvas_topics_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `canvas_user_meta` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `username` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `summary` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `avatar` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `dark_mode` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n  `digest_preference` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n  `locale` varchar(10) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `canvas_user_meta_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`id`) REFERENCES `users` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `canvas_views` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `post_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `ip_address` varchar(45) NOT NULL,\n  `user_agent` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `referer` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `canvas_views_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`post_id`) REFERENCES `canvas_posts` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `canvas_visits` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `post_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `ip_address` varchar(45) NOT NULL,\n  `user_agent` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `referer` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `canvas_visits_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`post_id`) REFERENCES `canvas_posts` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `users` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `email_verified` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n  `password` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  `updated_at` datetime NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
